            <ArticleTitle>The role of IgA and IgA Fc receptors as anti-inflammatory agents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S61-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10875-010-9397-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Serum monomeric immunoglobulin A (IgA) has powerful immunomodulatory roles in its ability to suppress immune responses. Monomeric IgA acts through FcalphaRI, which plays a significant role in vivo in maintaining the integrity of immune responses by an inhibitory immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif-mediated process and powerful inhibitory cellular effector mechanisms elicited by the IgA system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Triggering of FcalphaRI-mediated inhibitory function by Fab anti-FcalphaRI-mediated inhibitory function is able to inhibit several inflammatory and autoimmune diseases including asthma and glomerulonephritis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of monomeric IgA as a therapeutic tool in human inflammatory diseases may offer much potential.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
